## [§](#compatibility-notes)Compatibility Notes

Before v0.26.0, [`Line`](../prelude/struct.Line.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Line") did not have a `style` field and instead relied on only the styles that were set on each [`Span`](../prelude/struct.Span.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Span") contained in the `spans` field. The [`Line::patch_style`](../prelude/struct.Line.html#method.patch_style "method ratatui::prelude::Line::patch_style") method was the only way to set the overall style for individual lines. For this reason, this field may not be supported yet by all widgets (outside of the `ratatui` crate itself).

## [§](#examples)Examples

### [§](#creating-lines)Creating Lines

[`Line`](../prelude/struct.Line.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Line")s can be created from [`Span`](../prelude/struct.Span.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Span")s, [`String`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/string/struct.String.html "struct alloc::string::String")s, and [`&str`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/primitive.str.html "primitive str")s. They can be styled with a [`Style`](../prelude/struct.Style.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Style").

```
use ratatui_core::style::{Color, Modifier, Style, Stylize};
use ratatui_core::text::{Line, Span};

let style = Style::new().yellow();
let line = Line::raw("Hello, world!").style(style);
let line = Line::styled("Hello, world!", style);
let line = Line::styled("Hello, world!", (Color::Yellow, Modifier::BOLD));

let line = Line::from("Hello, world!");
let line = Line::from(String::from("Hello, world!"));
let line = Line::from(vec![
    Span::styled("Hello", Style::new().blue()),
    Span::raw(" world!"),
]);
```

### [§](#styling-lines)Styling Lines

The line’s [`Style`](../prelude/struct.Style.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Style") is used by the rendering widget to determine how to style the line. Each [`Span`](../prelude/struct.Span.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Span") in the line will be styled with the [`Style`](../prelude/struct.Style.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Style") of the line, and then with its own [`Style`](../prelude/struct.Style.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Style"). If the line is longer than the available space, the style is applied to the entire line, and the line is truncated. `Line` also implements [`Styled`](../style/trait.Styled.html "trait ratatui::style::Styled") which means you can use the methods of the [`Stylize`](../prelude/trait.Stylize.html "trait ratatui::prelude::Stylize") trait.

```
use ratatui_core::style::{Color, Modifier, Style, Stylize};
use ratatui_core::text::Line;

let line = Line::from("Hello world!").style(Style::new().yellow().italic());
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").style(Color::Yellow);
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").style((Color::Yellow, Color::Black));
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").style((Color::Yellow, Modifier::ITALIC));
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").yellow().italic();
```

### [§](#aligning-lines)Aligning Lines

The line’s [`Alignment`](../prelude/type.Alignment.html "type ratatui::prelude::Alignment") is used by the rendering widget to determine how to align the line within the available space. If the line is longer than the available space, the alignment is ignored and the line is truncated.

```
use ratatui_core::layout::Alignment;
use ratatui_core::text::Line;

let line = Line::from("Hello world!").alignment(Alignment::Right);
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").centered();
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").left_aligned();
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").right_aligned();
```

### [§](#rendering-lines)Rendering Lines

`Line` implements the [`Widget`](../prelude/trait.Widget.html "trait ratatui::prelude::Widget") trait, which means it can be rendered to a [`Buffer`](../prelude/struct.Buffer.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Buffer").

```
use ratatui_core::buffer::Buffer;
use ratatui_core::layout::Rect;
use ratatui_core::style::{Style, Stylize};
use ratatui_core::text::Line;
use ratatui_core::widgets::Widget;

// in another widget's render method
let line = Line::from("Hello world!").style(Style::new().yellow().italic());
line.render(area, buf);
```

Or you can use the `render_widget` method on the `Frame` in a `Terminal::draw` closure.

[ⓘ](# "This example is not tested")

```
let line = Line::from("Hello world!");
frame.render_widget(line, area);
```

### [§](#rendering-lines-with-a-paragraph-widget)Rendering Lines with a Paragraph widget

Usually apps will use the `Paragraph` widget instead of rendering a [`Line`](../prelude/struct.Line.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Line") directly as it provides more functionality.

[ⓘ](# "This example is not tested")

```
use ratatui::{
    buffer::Buffer,
    layout::Rect,
    style::Stylize,
    text::Line,
    widgets::{Paragraph, Widget, Wrap},
};

let line = Line::from("Hello world!").yellow().italic();
Paragraph::new(line)
    .wrap(Wrap { trim: true })
    .render(area, buf);
```

